 42	\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma \ref{lem:unique-operator-solution}]
 43		Let $\tau$ be an operator solution, i.e.\ a representation of $\G$ with $\tau(J) = \w_d I$. Let $\tau = \oplus_{i=1}^k\tau_i$ be a decomposition of $\tau$ into $k$ irreducibles. 
 44		As in Lemma \ref{fact:character-convex-combination}, let $\tilde \chi: g\mapsto \frac1{\dim \tau}\Tr\tau(g)$ be the normalized character of $\tau$ and $\tilde \chi_i$ be the same for $\tau_i$. One can check that $\abs{\tilde\chi_i(g)} \leq 1$ for all $g\in \G$. Furthermore, $\tilde\chi(g)$ is a convex combination of the $\chi_i(g)$. Therefore, $\tilde\chi_i(J) = \w_d$ for each $i$. Then also $\tau_i(J) = \w_dI$ for each $i$, since this the only $d$-dimensional unitary with trace $d\w_d$. We conclude that  $\tau$ is equivalent to $\bigoplus_{i=1}^k\s = \s\otimes I_k$. 
 45	
 46		Now suppose that $\tau'$ is a conjugate operator solution. Then taking the complex conjugate in any basis, $\bar{\tau'}$ is an operator solution. By the above, $\bar{\tau'}$ is equivalent to $\s\otimes I$. Therefore, $\tau'$ is equivalent to $\bar{\s}\otimes I$.
 47	\end{proof}
